Part D — Create a Simple Form
Now the second part of Practical 1 is to create a form that accepts a user's name.
For example, if you enter:
John

the application should display:
Welcome John

The PDF implements this using a GET Index() action, a POST Index(string name) action, and a Razor view.     prac 1
Step 10: Modify HomeController
Open:
Controllers
    → HomeController.cs

Replace the previous controller code with:
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace HelloWebApp.Controllers
{
    public class HomeController : Controller
    {
        public IActionResult Index()
        {
            return View();
        }

        [HttpPost]
        public IActionResult Index(string name)
        {
            ViewBag.Message = "Welcome " + name;
            return View();
        }
    }
}

Step 11: Understand the Controller Code
There are now two Index methods.
First method
public IActionResult Index()
{
    return View();
}

This displays the form.
Second method
[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Index(string name)
{
    ViewBag.Message = "Welcome " + name;
    return View();
}

This receives the name submitted from the form.
For example:
name = John

The following code:
ViewBag.Message = "Welcome " + name;

creates:
Welcome John

